WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.038 --> 00:00:05.358
Current government regulations require
about $98 billion to be

00:00:05.358 --> 00:00:07.458
on the books of our banks.

00:00:07.458 --> 00:00:10.678
We have a little more than that.

00:00:10.678 --> 00:00:15.678
We have $2.4 trillion in excess of our reserves.

00:00:15.678 --> 00:00:18.298
I think that's a lot of money.

00:00:18.298 --> 00:00:21.358
You could do a lot of work with that money,

00:00:21.358 --> 00:00:24.678
if that money got invested into businesses.

00:00:24.678 --> 00:00:26.958
What does that mean in terms of scale?

00:00:26.958 --> 00:00:31.178
It's about two-thirds of the entire federal budget.

00:00:31.178 --> 00:00:36.018
That is a lot of money just sitting there just like it

00:00:36.018 --> 00:00:38.018
did in the Great Depression.

00:00:38.018 --> 00:00:40.138
But where do banks have their money? Maybe

00:00:40.138 --> 00:00:42.258
their money is already tied up in risky

00:00:42.258 --> 00:00:44.398
business ventures. They don't want anymore.

00:00:44.398 --> 00:00:48.578
Is that the case?  No, that's also not the case.

00:00:48.578 --> 00:00:53.478
This is a graph of business loans and consumer loans.

00:00:53.478 --> 00:00:55.618
I checked this 16 different ways.

00:00:55.618 --> 00:00:58.418
I couldn't believe it when I saw this.

00:00:58.418 --> 00:01:05.578
That right now consumer debt is around $3.5 trillion.

00:01:05.578 --> 00:01:10.098
And this is business loans, this is like negligible.

00:01:10.098 --> 00:01:13.298
It's a few hundred billion dollars.

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:17.118
97% less. I don't know about you,

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:21.113
but this is not how capitalism is supposed to work.

00:01:21.118 --> 00:01:23.638
Those are supposed to be flip-flopped.

00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:26.298
That's how economies grow.

00:01:26.298 --> 00:01:28.598
They don't grow through consumer debt.

00:01:28.598 --> 00:01:31.958
But if you're curious about how this happened,

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:37.258
there is a book, that I wrote, called "Borrow",

00:01:37.258 --> 00:01:39.598
that I can tell you about how that happened.

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:44.858
But basically the idea is that it became much easier

00:01:44.858 --> 00:01:48.638
to securitize all this consumer debt,

00:01:48.638 --> 00:01:51.238
to bundle it up and sell it on capital markets,

00:01:51.238 --> 00:01:53.298
than it is for business debt.

00:01:53.298 --> 00:01:55.898
In fact it's not possible really to do that now.

00:01:55.898 --> 00:01:59.698
In fact our largest government agency

00:01:59.698 --> 00:02:01.718
that's supposed to be in charge of doing this

00:02:01.718 --> 00:02:03.758
doing what we imagine--what the

00:02:03.758 --> 00:02:07.398
Reconstruction Finance Corporation did in the 01930s

00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:12.198
the Small Business Administration

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.426
does hardly any of it.

00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:16.598
So $15 billion--this is like a drop in the bucket

00:02:16.598 --> 00:02:19.918
compared to either the size of banks

00:02:19.918 --> 00:02:23.858
or even the entire market for small business loans

00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:27.498
if you include real estate. Why is this?

00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:33.178
It's because we've had government
policies in the 1960s and 70s

00:02:33.178 --> 00:02:36.618
that made it possible to securitize consumer debt.

00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:41.238
To make it easier for banks and lenders and investors

00:02:41.238 --> 00:02:43.298
to put money into your credit cards

00:02:43.298 --> 00:02:45.518
than into your small business.

00:02:45.518 --> 00:02:48.318
Than into even your large business.

00:02:48.318 --> 00:02:52.258
And this is from a report from the Federal Reserve.

00:02:52.258 --> 00:02:56.030
A report to Congress. Why it's not possible to do this.

00:02:56.038 --> 00:02:58.758
Now I think if it's possible to hide Greek debt

00:02:58.758 --> 00:03:01.818
by Goldman Sachs. If it's possible for all those

00:03:01.818 --> 00:03:03.718
wizards of Wall Street to do all the magic

00:03:03.718 --> 00:03:06.038
they do every day, we can probably do

00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:08.258
for small business debt what we did for

00:03:08.258 --> 00:03:12.317
for houses in Texas in the 01930s (before they

00:03:12.318 --> 00:03:14.958
had computers or... you know anything really).

00:03:14.958 --> 00:03:17.858
They're just primitive, cavemen...

00:03:19.058 --> 00:03:21.458
That's point one.

00:03:21.718 --> 00:03:26.438
Point two: it would not fix the new leading sector.

00:03:26.438 --> 00:03:28.998
How do we find this new leading sector?

00:03:28.998 --> 00:03:31.818
For instance we're all excited about self-driving cars...

00:03:31.818 --> 00:03:33.258
Who's excited about self-driving cars?

00:03:33.258 --> 00:03:35.198
I'm excited about self-driving cars.

00:03:35.198 --> 00:03:39.158
I hate driving. I like sleeping and reading.

00:03:39.158 --> 00:03:41.238
Now this will create a lot of demand

00:03:41.238 --> 00:03:42.998
I'm very excited, this is a good thing.

00:03:42.998 --> 00:03:44.718
It will create a lot of demand though.

00:03:44.718 --> 00:03:46.898
Not just for the cars, the replacement of all our cars,

00:03:46.898 --> 00:03:49.398
but also the rebuilding of our cities. The rebuilding

00:03:49.398 --> 00:03:51.798
of our entire built environment around these cars.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:54.358
A lot of connections. A lot of growth.

00:03:54.358 --> 00:03:56.198
This is the kind of thing that

00:03:56.217 --> 00:03:59.268
could be part of a new leading sector.

00:03:59.268 --> 00:04:00.769
It is the right direction.

00:04:00.778 --> 00:04:04.978
Now the other thing I want to talk about with that is...

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:08.218
that sounded crazy... how many years ago?

00:04:08.218 --> 00:04:12.498
Two? 3 years ago? If I was here five years ago,

00:04:12.498 --> 00:04:14.758
and I was like: "Hey, I'm making a self-driving car"

00:04:14.758 --> 00:04:17.558
You'd be like "Yeah, good luck with your rocket boots!"

00:04:17.558 --> 00:04:21.858
And yet it's possible. It's a failure of imagination.

00:04:21.858 --> 00:04:25.738
In fact we see this all the time in our society.

00:04:25.738 --> 00:04:28.038
A failure of imagination.

00:04:28.038 --> 00:04:30.958
Which stems from a basic failure of science.

00:04:30.958 --> 00:04:32.938
Which I'm going to come back to.

00:04:32.938 --> 00:04:36.918
We live in an era that is not disruptive

00:04:36.918 --> 00:04:40.618
but incrementalist. Bear with me.

00:04:40.618 --> 00:04:43.558
Most of the science we build our technology on

00:04:43.558 --> 00:04:46.078
the basic scientific principles (electricity,

00:04:46.078 --> 00:04:49.038
quantum mechanics) have resulted in products

00:04:49.038 --> 00:04:51.418
that have been refined since the 1960s

00:04:51.418 --> 00:04:54.298
Since the 1950s even in a lot of ways.

00:04:54.298 --> 00:04:58.558
Incrementalist. Note, this room

00:04:58.558 --> 00:05:00.898
basically would look the same 50 years ago.

00:05:00.898 --> 00:05:04.298
Whereas 50 years before that none of it would exist.

00:05:04.298 --> 00:05:07.258
We think we live in a time of technological change,

00:05:07.258 --> 00:05:10.818
but it's actually relatively slow.

00:05:10.818 --> 00:05:13.238
Because we have cut off imagination

00:05:13.238 --> 00:05:15.898
and have cut off innovative science.

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.828
Now a good test of what a new leading sector would be:

00:05:18.828 --> 00:05:22.938
does it sound as crazy as a self-driving car?

00:05:22.938 --> 00:05:25.518
What are some of the kinds of crazy things

00:05:25.518 --> 00:05:28.718
you could imagine in the future? I named rocket boots.

00:05:28.718 --> 00:05:31.258
Those sounds totally insane.

00:05:31.258 --> 00:05:34.058
I want to point out one thing that also...

00:05:34.058 --> 00:05:37.018
10 years ago when I was talking about this with my wife

00:05:37.018 --> 00:05:38.098
and we were in grad school,

00:05:38.098 --> 00:05:40.138
I was like self-driving cars

00:05:40.138 --> 00:05:44.138
and space elevators. Space elevators, right?

00:05:44.138 --> 00:05:48.098
Those sound crazy, right? Yeah! A big strip

00:05:48.098 --> 00:05:52.498
of material into space with an elevator attached?

00:05:52.498 --> 00:05:56.838
That's ludicrous. But imagine what it would be like.

00:05:56.838 --> 00:05:58.858
It's the right order of thinking.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.471
Something totally, totally new.

00:06:01.471 --> 00:06:03.415
A platform for other industries.

00:06:03.415 --> 00:06:05.237
Radical new materials science.

00:06:05.238 --> 00:06:09.678
And most importantly millions and millions of jobs.

00:06:09.678 --> 00:06:12.158
As a platform for new possibilities

00:06:12.158 --> 00:06:15.078
And again I am NOT an industrialist.

00:06:15.078 --> 00:06:16.558
I am NOT a scientist even.

00:06:16.558 --> 00:06:18.098
So I have no stake in this.

00:06:18.098 --> 00:06:20.878
If this happens I will not make any money.

00:06:20.878 --> 00:06:22.858
I would not even be allowed in space.

00:06:22.858 --> 00:06:25.418
Because my wife thinks I'm going to die there.

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:26.578
 

00:06:33.718 --> 00:06:35.778
 

